<edge source="mideast_iran_us_conflict::supply_disruption_risk" target="global_financial_tension::long_term_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A rise in the assessed probability of energy‑supply disruptions raises expected future inflation and adds a term‑risk premium, directly pushing up long‑maturity bond yields as investors demand compensation for higher expected price and tail risks.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">mideast_iran_us_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">global_financial_tension</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mideast_iran_us_conflict::supply_disruption_risk" target="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_severity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">An elevated assessed risk of Middle East supply disruptions causes immediate global price shocks and reallocation of LNG/oil cargoes away from Europe, directly increasing the magnitude and likelihood of energy shortfalls tied to the Russia–Ukraine war.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">mideast_iran_us_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mideast_iran_us_conflict::supply_disruption_risk" target="us_china_ai_tech_rivalry::tech_geopolitical_tensio" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">A rising assessed risk of energy and transit disruption from an Iran–US confrontation prompts the US and allied governments to adopt export controls, investment screening and supply‑chain security measures that directly harden technology trade frictions with China.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">mideast_iran_us_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_ai_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mideast_iran_us_conflict::supply_disruption_risk" target="us_trade_protectionism::trade_barrier_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Rising perceived risk of energy or commodity interruptions leads governments to impose export controls, emergency tariffs or quotas and other protective measures to conserve domestic supplies, increasing effective trade barriers.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">mideast_iran_us_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_trade_protectionism</data>
</edge>
<edge source="mideast_iran_us_conflict::supply_disruption_risk" target="us_trade_protectionism::asset_price_volatility" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.9</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher expected probability and severity of supply disruptions raises uncertainty about commodity prices and corporate earnings, prompting greater price swings across equity, credit and commodity markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">mideast_iran_us_conflict</data>
  <data key="d40">us_trade_protectionism</data>
