<edge source="us_china_ai_tech_rivalry::semiconductor_capacity" target="global_financial_tension::long_term_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A meaningful shortfall in semiconductor manufacturing capacity raises expectations of persistent supply constraints and sectoral price pressure, increasing inflation risk premia embedded in long-term bond yields.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_ai_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">global_financial_tension</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_ai_tech_rivalry::semiconductor_capacity" target="mideast_iran_us_conflict::oil_export_flow_volume" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained increase (or decrease) in advanced semiconductor manufacturing capacity—especially in major Asian economies—changes industrial and data‑centre activity and therefore short‑to‑medium‑term oil demand, which translates into higher (or lower) volumes of crude and refined exports transiting Middle East sea lanes within months.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_ai_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">mideast_iran_us_conflict</data>
</edge>
<edge source="us_china_ai_tech_rivalry::semiconductor_capacity" target="us_trade_protectionism::supply_chain_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Incremental increases in domestic or allied semiconductor manufacturing capacity relieve foundry bottlenecks and inventory shortages, directly reducing the frequency and severity of supply‑chain disruptions for chip‑dependent goods.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">us_china_ai_tech_rivalry</data>
  <data key="d40">us_trade_protectionism</data>
</edge>
